What you will be able to do
By the end of this practical you should be able to:

pull out the rows and columns you want, and sort a table;
create a new variable by recoding an existing one;
set the order of a factor’s levels, and say why that changes a model;
summarise a variable within each diagnostic group;
draw a histogram, a boxplot and a scatterplot, with axes a reader can understand;
turn a wide table into a long one, which is the shape repeated-measures analyses need.







Note⬇ Download this practical



A zip containing the R script, the data, and an .Rproj file. Unzip it somewhere you can find again, then double-click the .Rproj.

What you will be able to do

name the atomic types, and predict what happens when you mix them;
build a vector, subset it every way there is, and replace parts of it;
explain why c(1, 2, 3, 4) + c(10, 20) gives an answer rather than an error;
use which(), any(), all() and %in%;
tell NA from NaN from Inf;
see that a matrix and a factor are just vectors with attributes attached;
build a factor with the levels in the order you want;
do matrix arithmetic, including the difference between * and %*%.

What you will be able to do

use R as a calculator, and recognise when floating-point arithmetic has tricked you;
create objects, and say what &lt;- does that = does not;
find your way around the help system;
see what is in your workspace, and clear it;
set up a working directory properly, and read a data file from it;
run str() on anything and read what it tells you.

Naming things
Names may be any length, are case sensitive, and may contain letters, digits, . and _. They must start with a letter (or a dot not followed by a digit).
The lecture’s style advice:

stick to ASCII, so your code travels;
start with letters, put numbers at the end;
use _ to separate words — snake_case or camelCase;
you can use . as a separator, but better to avoid it;
use names that make sense.


reaction_time &lt;- 412      # good
rt2 &lt;- 412                # fine







Warning



Do not name a variable after a function you use — mean, c, data, df, T, F. T &lt;- FALSE is legal and will quietly break things later.

What you will be able to do

build a list, and pick things out of it with [, [[ and $;
explain why a data frame is a list, and what follows from that;
read the output of str() on a fitted model;
write if, ifelse() and for, and say when you do not need a loop;
write a function, with arguments and a return value.
